WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.465
Levels 4 and 5 broadly represent enterprise planning, business systems, corporate users, and external connectivity.

00:00:07.465 --> 00:00:19.455
Organizations describe these levels differently, but common examples include enterprise resource planning, corporate identity, finance, email, user workstations, data platforms, internet services, and cloud connections.

00:00:19.455 --> 00:00:23.301
These systems support the business around production.

00:00:23.301 --> 00:00:30.314
They create schedules, manage materials and orders, provide workforce identities, and consume operational data.

00:00:30.314 --> 00:00:43.662
Because they serve many users and connect to external services, they are exposed to common enterprise threats such as phishing, credential compromise, ransomware, vulnerable applications, and third-party access.

00:00:43.662 --> 00:00:48.412
Enterprise compromise can create OT risk through shared dependencies.

00:00:48.412 --> 00:00:53.163
A corporate identity outage may affect authorized remote support.

00:00:53.163 --> 00:00:58.366
Ransomware may disrupt production scheduling or files needed by operations.

00:00:58.366 --> 00:01:04.022
A compromised user account may reach a poorly controlled jump host.

00:01:04.022 --> 00:01:10.583
A cloud integration may create a path that bypasses the intended industrial DMZ.

00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:17.143
The Purdue view helps teams resist direct connections that are convenient but unsafe.

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:21.894
Enterprise applications should exchange required data through controlled services.

00:01:21.894 --> 00:01:25.740
Corporate users should not administer controllers directly.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:34.110
Identity and remote-access designs should preserve strong governance without creating a single unexamined dependency for critical operations.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:41.123
Security teams should map cross-level business flows, including scheduling, reporting, support, updates, and authentication.

00:01:41.123 --> 00:01:47.684
For each flow, identify what happens if it is unavailable, altered, or misused.

00:01:47.684 --> 00:01:52.435
Enterprise and OT teams share responsibility for these boundaries.

00:01:52.435 --> 00:02:05.330
Protecting Level 4 and Level 5 is part of OT risk reduction because the enterprise environment often supplies the pathways, identities, and services that connect to operations.
